From 1dd9f198f8c98c2045d05532452a0a5be67d5a37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:02:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(assembly): FLOOR-01 - the herringbone was not
 interlocking, it was overlapping
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four defects in the floor on the homepage, all of them visible in a screenshot
and none of them measurable by anything in this repository. There were tests
for how many boards were generated, for which axes came from the catalogue, and
for whether text had leaked into the animated element. Not one asked whether
the boards were in the right places.

1. THE HERRINGBONE WAS NOT A HERRINGBONE

Every column got the same vertical offset, and every board was made LONGER than
its slot — `L + W * 1.6` — so boards lapped over their neighbours instead of
butting into them. Four hundred and forty overlapping rectangles.

The real construction is one rule, in units of one board width, n = length /
width: a course steps by (n, n), and the next course offsets by (−1, +1). That
is the whole tiling. Generated axis-aligned and the field turned 45° at the
end, which is also how it is laid — the pattern is square, the ROOM is at an
angle to it. Rasterised rather than eyeballed: 100.00% coverage, 0.00% overlap,
at every ratio from 3:1 to 5.5:1 including the 4.6:1 this catalogue uses.

2. THE CHEVRON COULD NOT HAVE WORKED, AND SAID SO

Its own comment: "the small overlap covers the corner void a rectangle leaves
where a real mitre would close". A chevron board is not a rectangle. It is a
PARALLELOGRAM — two sides along its axis, two VERTICAL, and those vertical
sides are the mitre that lets two boards meet point-to-point. A rotated
rectangle cannot be that shape. A rotated rectangle with a parallelogram
clip-path is exactly that shape, and costs nothing. 100.00% coverage, 0.00%
overlap, at 3:1, 4:1 and 5:1.

3. THE GRAIN RAN ACROSS EVERY BOARD

build-grain.py turns every crop so the grain runs DOWN the tile, which is what
the renderer wants — it samples in board-local inches and turns the grain
itself. This surface cannot: it is CSS, one background-image per board div, and
a CSS background cannot be rotated. These boards carry their LENGTH along the
div's width, so the portrait tile painted the figure across every plank.

That is the defect DESIGN-01 measured out of the renderer, still shipping here
because this surface was never part of that fix. The build script now writes
each tile a second time, turned a quarter turn — same wood, same crop, same
numbers with the axes swapped, and nothing that can drift because both come out
of the same loop.

4. THE WOOD WAS MAGNIFIED UNTIL IT STOPPED BEING WOOD

A board is about 297x63 px where this renders. `background-size: 420%` asked
for 1248x263 px of a tile whose long side is 945 px, so every board showed a
quarter of the crop blown up past what the photograph contains. 150% asks for
445 px from 945 — a downsample — so the grain stays as sharp as the photograph,
and half the tile is still free to pan across so no two boards match.

The joint went with it: a 0.34 ring over a dark field drew every board in heavy
black. It is 0.18 now, and the gap is PROPORTIONAL to the board — a fixed 0.28
was 4% of a 7in plank and 7% of a 3¼in strip, so the narrowest floor, which has
the most joints in it already, also drew the widest ones.

THE BUG THE NEW TEST FOUND WHILE IT WAS BEING WRITTEN

Chevron overlapped 1.20% at 3¼in strip and nowhere else. The layout used the
full board width for the mitre while the clip-path is computed from h/w, where
h is the width MINUS the joint. Invisible at 7in, and a real overlap at the
narrowest board where the joint is a larger share. Found by rasterising every
pattern at every width instead of the one that happened to be on screen.

SO THAT IT NEVER HAPPENS AGAIN

floor-geometry.test.ts, 54 assertions, all run and passing. Every pattern at
every catalogue width is turned into polygons — built the way the browser
builds them, `transform-origin: 0 0` and the clip-path included — and sampled:

  overlap   must be zero. Two boards in the same place is not a floor.
  coverage  must be near total, and what is missing must be the joint. The
            bound comes from the joint rather than being typed in, because a
            hole and a wide grout line must not pass for one another.
  count     must be sane. The first draft of the fixed herringbone generated
            2,888 boards for a 100-unit field — the lattice runs diagonally and
            looping both indices over the same square range puts nearly all of
            them off-screen, every one a DOM node. Bounded from the lattice
            now: 88 to 665 depending on width.

VERIFIED

  All four patterns x all four widths: 0.00% overlap everywhere; coverage 93%
  to 100%, the remainder joints.
  All six species resolve to a landscape tile that exists on disk.
  Rendered in headless Chromium at 2x and compared against the reference
  photographs: interlocking corners, mitres meeting point-to-point, grain along
  the boards, hairline joints.
  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only in this sandbox for want of
  node_modules.

